  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1023 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nRain to Snow Saturday through Monday...\nDry conditions will come to an end Saturday as slow moving cold\nfront and upcoming mid-level trough cross the area. There remains\nsome uncertainty on the exact onset of the initial showers, with\nsome models producing an early afternoon round of rainfall in the\nwarm air advection area over central Wisconsin; while other \nmodels hold off until the actual passage of the cold front in the \nlate afternoon. Included some 30-40% pops for the early afternoon\nacross central to north-central Wisconsin to include this\npotential before increasing quickly to 60-90% during the late\nafternoon and evening. That said, the potential for rain across\neast-central Wisconsin, including the Fox Valley, does remain\nlower until the overnight hours, not increasing until the hours\naround midnight. This suggests a slower passage of the cold front\ninitially further east and does play into the precipitation\namounts, lowering them slightly in the east and increasing the\nhigher end of portions of central and northern Wisconsin. \nProbability of at least half an inch of rainfall over these east- \ncentral areas sits at around 30-40% but increases quickly to \n60-80% over the rest of central and northern Wisconsin.\nProbability of exceeding an inch of rainfall over the course of 24\nhours still sits around 50-60% for areas of central to northern \nWisconsin. No thunder is currently in the forecast to accompany\nthe rain. Finally, as the colder air starts to work in behind the\nrainfall, expect some snowfall to work its way into the region,\nmainly across far northern Wisconsin.By Monday evening, snowfall \namounts for 0.5\" are focused mainly in Vilas County with \nprobabilities of 15- 30% across most of the county and up to 70% \nalong the Upper MI border. As a final note, winds accompanying the\ncold front will make for a fairly breezy period Saturday and\nSunday, with gusts up to 25 mph at times.\n\nFollowing this system, dry conditions are expected to return and\nlast through the Thanksgiving holiday.\n\nTemperatures...Well above normal temps continue tonight with lows\nranging from the upper 40s to low 50s, leading to a few locations\npotentially setting new record high minimums tonight as well. \nGiven the slower arrival of the cold front, high temperatures will\nremain well above normal on Saturday with highs ranging from the \nupper 50s to mid 60s. Several locations may also be flirting with \nrecord highs. The arrival of the rain/cold front will result in \ncooling temperatures into Sunday with highs in the low 40s to low \n50s. Temperatures will then fall to near normal for the start of \nnext work week with highs in the 30s and 40s.",
